#!spectest
# mode: run
# adjudicator: production-v1
# since: spec-v0.1
# features: [move, callable, loop]
# doc: |
#   RV-F #15 param-gate (single-owner Callable, REJECT direction — the for-var
#   half). Binding `Callable g = f` bare is ACCEPTED when `f` is a param
#   (callable_param_rebind_ok.gg) but REJECTED when `f` is a `for`-element callable:
#   a for-var is Borrow-mode but is NOT a param, and the single-owner-init gate
#   skips ONLY params (production `!def.is_param`, check_stmt.rs:1464) — so the bare
#   copy is E_MoveWithoutOperator. This is the REJECT half of the param-specific
#   gate: together with the param ACCEPT it proves the gate keys on param-ness, not
#   on Borrow-mode (which also marks for-vars). The reject fires at the bind before
#   any iteration runs.
#
#   adjudicator: production-v1 — ELABORATE-stage reject (GGDEF-SKIP on the ggdef
#   run-surface lane; C/LLVM affirm error[E_MoveWithoutOperator] at build).
#
#   KNOWN SELF-HOST GAP (filed, TODO.md): the self-host does not treat `Callable`
#   as single-owner, so it ACCEPTS this for-var bind — the self-host lane
#   MISMATCHes. The expected verdict is what the language SHOULD do (reject); the
#   fixture EXPOSES the gap.
# expect:
#   exit: 1
#   stdout: ""
#   reject: E_MoveWithoutOperator
#!end

void main():
    Vector[Callable[int()]] fns = [(): 1, (): 2]
    for f in fns:
        Callable[int()] g = f
        print(g())
